---
// Editor: the panel's own web page (workbench/EDITOR-V1.md, the v1 scope). The pictures are the clickable mock
// (workbench /wasm/editor-v1, captured into public/editor/); each is replaced by a panel screenshot as its part lands.
import Docs, { type Heading } from '../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/nav';

const headings: Heading[] = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'open', text: 'Opening it' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'screens', text: 'Screens' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'screen-settings', text: "A screen's settings" },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'panel-settings', text: 'Panel settings' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'send', text: 'Send, check, undo' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'not-yet', text: 'Not in this version' },
];
const pic = (name: string, alt: string) => ({ src: url(`/editor/${name}.png`), alt });
---

<Docs edit="site/src/pages/editor.astro" title="Editor" description="The panel's own web page: choose and order its screens, set each one up, set the panel, then send, check and undo." group="Use Facet" current={url('/editor/')} headings={headings}>
  <p class="lede">
    Every Facet panel serves its own editor. Open the panel's address in a browser to choose its screens and their
    order, set each one up and set the panel, then send it all and see the panel check it. Nothing else is needed: no
    add-on, no hub, the same page on both panels.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <p>The editor is being built for Facet 1.0. The pictures here are from its design mock; each is replaced by a screenshot from a panel as that part lands.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="open">Opening it</h2>
  <p>
    Type the panel's address into a browser on the same network: <code>http://facet-</code> and the six characters on
    the panel's Set up screen, then <code>.local/</code>, for example <code>http://facet-1a2b3c.local/</code>. The page
    talks to the panel directly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="screens">Screens</h2>
  <figure class="editor-shot">
    <img {...pic('screens', 'The Screens list: Home first, then each screen with a live thumbnail, its data source, up and down buttons and an on / off switch')} width="1200" height="820" loading="lazy" />
  </figure>
  <p>
    Every screen the panel has, plus Home, in one list. Each row has a live thumbnail, where its data comes from, an
    on / off switch, and a handle to drag it into place (up and down buttons too). The order is the order you swipe
    through and the order of Home's cards. A screen that is off stays in its place, greyed, and is left off the panel.
    Home and the start page cannot be turned off. The start page is Home unless you pick another.
  </p>

  <h2 id="screen-settings">A screen's settings</h2>
  <figure class="editor-shot">
    <img {...pic('screen-settings', "One screen's settings: its preview, large, beside its settings")} width="1200" height="820" loading="lazy" />
  </figure>
  <p>
    Tap a screen to see it large beside its settings. An entity is picked from the panel's own Home Assistant, a list of
    entities is a multiple choice, a short choice is a row of buttons. The preview follows every change. <strong>Back to
    defaults</strong> undoes a screen's settings.
  </p>

  <h2 id="panel-settings">Panel settings</h2>
  <figure class="editor-shot">
    <img {...pic('panel-settings', 'Panel settings: the Home Assistant link, the Home header buttons and the look, with Home previewed')} width="1200" height="820" loading="lazy" />
  </figure>
  <p>
    The panel's link to Home Assistant comes first: its address and a long-lived token, typed once on this page and sent
    straight to the panel. The token is never shown again, only its last four characters. Then the buttons at the top of
    Home, and the panel's settings in their groups: Look, Sound, Clock and idle, Behaviour, Units and AI agents. The
    preview is Home with the look applied.
  </p>

  <h2 id="send">Send, check, undo</h2>
  <figure class="editor-shot">
    <img {...pic('review', 'Review before sending: each change in words, was and now, and the panel ready to send')} width="1200" height="820" loading="lazy" />
  </figure>
  <p>
    The top bar counts your changes. <strong>Review and send</strong> lists each one in words, what it was and what it
    becomes, and the panel checks them before anything is sent. A problem is named next to the screen or setting it is
    about. If the panel refuses a send, your edits stay. <strong>Undo last send</strong> puts back what the panel had
    before; <strong>Back to factory</strong> returns it to the screens it came with.
  </p>

  <h2 id="not-yet">Not in this version</h2>
  <p>
    Building your own screens from parts, editing themes and changing Home's card sizes come later. Until then, screens
    of your own can be made as specs: see <a href={url('/ai/')}>Make screens with AI</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>

<style>
  .editor-shot {
    margin: 16px 0;
  }
  .editor-shot img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--facet-color-line);
    border-radius: 12px;
  }
</style>
